  "live_records": {
    "q-334938": {
      "dom_id": "step-q334938",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Given that $J(t)={\\int }_{1}^{3}(5x+t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${J}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${J}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${J}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nJ(t)={\\int }_{1}^{3}(5x+t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{J}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(5x+t)^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(5x+t)^{2}] & =2(5x+t)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(5x+t) \\\\  & =2(5x+t)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2(5x+t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}2(5x+t)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{3}(5x+t)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(5x+t)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}5x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{3}t\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{1}^{3}+tx{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =20+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}^{′}(t) & =2(20+2t) \\\\  & =4(t+10).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
