    "q-352881": {
      "dom_id": "step-q352881",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=6{e}^{2t}(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{3}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{3}] & =3({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{2}\\cdot 2{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =6{e}^{2t}({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{e}^{2t}({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{e}^{2t}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}{e}^{2t}+{e}^{4t})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{2}{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{4t}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}{|}_{0}^{1}+{e}^{2t}\\cdot \\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{0}^{1}+{e}^{4t}\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{2t}+{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=6{e}^{2t}(\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{2t}+{e}^{4t}).\n$$",
